[Line "6.a3 c4: 7.Nbd2 Na5 8.g3 (main line)"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.a3: the main line"]
[Summary "White takes b4 away from Black and prepares b2-b4. Black closes the queenside with ...c4; White regroups with Nbd2 and g3 and goes for h4, Bh3 and a kingside squeeze."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 { [%cal Rc7c5,Rf7f6] White takes space and denies f6 to the knight. In return Black gets
two levers against the chain: ...c5 against its base on d4, and ...f6 against its head on e5. The French bishop
sits behind its own pawns on e6 and d5; the whole opening is an argument about whether that matters. }
3... c5 { The critical reply. 3...c5 attacks the base of the chain, and if Black ever removes d4, the pawn on e5 is
loose. The rare third moves have their own file. } 4. c3 { [%cal Gc3d4,Gd1a4] The main move: if Black takes on d4,
the pawn recaptures and the chain stays intact, and the queen gets the a4-e8 diagonal. }
( 4. dxc5?! { The Steinitz Variation gives Black what it wants: the centre is gone and the e5-pawn has no pawn
behind it. Black wins the pawn back with ...Bxc5 while developing. } 4... Nc6 5. Nf3 Bxc5 6. Bd3 f6 7. Qe2 fxe5
8. Nxe5 Nxe5 9. Qxe5 Nf6 )
4... Nc6 { Second attacker on d4. 4...Qb6 and 4...Bd7 are move-order tries with their own lines. }
5. Nf3 { [%cal Gf3d4,Rc6d4] The third defender of d4 (pawn on c3, queen, knight), and the knight also guards e5.
5.Be3 is a rare try: after ...Qb6 the b2-pawn has no defender. }
( 5. Bb5?! { The bishop will have to trade itself for the knight or lose a tempo retreating. } 5... cxd4 6. Qxd4 Bd7
7. Bxc6 Bxc6 )
5... Qb6 { [%cal Rb6d4,Rb6b2] The main line (Wikipedia, Werle, Schneider). Queen and knight pile onto d4, and
b2 is under fire, so the dark-squared bishop cannot simply come to e3 or f4. White now has three real choices:
6.a3, 6.Be2 and the gambit 6.Bd3. }
6. a3 { [%cal Gb2b4,Gc1b2] **The idea:** a3 takes b4 away from the black bishop and knight, and prepares b2-b4,
after which the c1-bishop is free of the b2-pawn and d4 gains a defender (Bb2). It looks like a slow move with
the centre under attack, but d4 holds for the moment and Black is the one who now has to choose a structure.
Werle's and Schneider's main recommendation, and treated as the most important line in the Advance by
Wikipedia. }
6... c4 { [%csl Yb3] The main line. Black stops b4: after b2-b4 the pawn takes en passant on b3, and the clamp on
the queenside shuts in the f1-bishop. The price is d4, which is no longer under pressure from the c-pawn. }
7. Nbd2 { [%cal Gd2b3,Rc6a5] The move in Marin's course, Wikibooks and the studies. It covers b3, the square where
Black wants a piece, and can head for the kingside once the queenside is closed. }
7... Na5 { Black aims for b3, and with ...Bd7 for a4. }
( 7... Nge7 { A natural developing move, but it allows a sharp shot from the study with 395 likes. } 8. Bxc4! { After
...dxc4 the knight takes with a double attack on the queen and the knight on a5, and Nd6+ follows. The source gives the
line without an evaluation, so treat it as a tactic to know, not as a proven win. } 8... dxc4 9. Nxc4 Qa6
10. Nd6+ Kd7 ( 10... Kd8 11. Nxf7+ Kc7 12. Nxh8 ) 11. Ng5 Ng6 12. Ndxf7 Rg8 )
8. g3 { [%cal Gf1g2,Gf1h3,Gh2h4] The sharp choice (Marin gives it two chapters, and the study with 395 likes plays it).
Instead of Be2, where the bishop sits behind the pawn on c4, it goes to g2 or to h3. White's kingside pawns are
free to advance, since Black's pieces are busy on the other wing. }
( 8. Be2 { The quiet alternative (the 73-likes 'French Defense Repertoire' study, Wikibooks, Marin chapter 9) and a
perfectly good one: White castles and plays Rb1 at once. } 8... Bd7 9. O-O Ne7 10. Rb1 Qc7 )
( 8. Rb1 { Mentioned by Wikipedia, who adds that Black has a firm grip on b3 after ...Bd7; Marin lists it as a
sideline. } 8... Bd7 )
8... Bd7 { The bishop develops and prepares ...Ba4. }
9. h4 { [%cal Gh4h5,Gf1h3] **Plan:** h4-h5 gains space and restricts the black knights, and Bh3 offers a trade of the
d7-bishop, Black's piece for the queenside play. }
( 9. Bg2 { The slower version, also analysed by Marin: the bishop sits on the long diagonal and h4 can wait. } 9... Ne7
10. O-O h6 )
9... Ne7 10. Bh3 { Offering the exchange on d7. } 10... h6 { Stops Bg5. } 11. O-O Qc7 { Marin's chapter 11 ends with this
move, and the sources stop here. **Plan:** White has the kingside space and can think about opening the centre with
Re1, Nf1-e3 and f4; Black has the b-file and the squares b3 and a4, and ...b5-b4 is his lever.

**Verdict:** a closed, strategic fight in which both sides have a plan. the sources give no evaluation. }
*

[Line "Lputian Variation: 6...Nh6 7.b4"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.a3: the main line"]
[Summary "Black brings the knight to f5 without blocking the c8-bishop. White does not take on h6; he plays b4 and meets the knight with Bb2, with three defenders against three attackers on d4."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. a3 Nh6 { [%cal Gh6f5,Rf5d4] The Lputian Variation. The knight
heads for f5 and a third attacker on d4, from h6 rather than e7, so the f8-bishop keeps its diagonal to c5.
Wikipedia adds that Black follows with ...Bg7 and castles. }
7. b4! { [%cal Gc1b2,Gb4c5] The answer given by every source (Wikibooks, Schneider, the study with 395 likes and the 73-likes
'French Defense Repertoire' study). The pawn takes c5 under control and frees the bishop for b2, from where it defends
d4. }
( 7. Bxh6? { The tempting way to wreck Black's pawns. } 7... Qxb2! { Wikibooks: the queen takes the pawn with a threat on the
rook; after 8.Nbd2 Black recaptures on h6 and is two pawns up, with the g-file as compensation for the damaged
pawns. Wikipedia also says that Black is happy with the open g-file and ...Bg7. } 8. Nbd2 gxh6 )
7... cxd4 8. cxd4 Nf5 { [%cal Gf5d4,Gc6d4,Gb6d4] Three attackers on d4: queen, c6-knight, f5-knight. }
9. Bb2 { [%cal Gb2d4,Gf3d4] Now three defenders as well: the knight, the bishop and the queen. The pawn is
safe as long as all three stay put, so castling and development must not loosen it. } 9... Bd7 { The sources stop
here (the study with 395 likes; the 73-likes study also plays 9.Bb2 Bd7). **Plan:** keep the three defenders of d4
in place while developing the king's side; Black's ...a5 is the natural way to attack the pawn on b4.

**Verdict:** Wikibooks marks the Lputian line equal. White has the space, Black the pressure on d4; do not expect
more than that, and do not take on h6. } *

[Line "6.a3: 6...cxd4 7.cxd4 Nge7 8.b4 Nf5 9.Bb2"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.a3: the main line"]
[Summary "Black releases the tension at once. White recaptures with the pawn, expands with b4 and plays on both wings; the knights meet on the kingside."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. a3 cxd4 { Black resolves the tension before White's b4. }
7. cxd4 { [%cal Gc3b5] Always with the pawn: the e5-pawn would be loose after a knight recapture. }
( 7. Nxd4? { Takes the piece away from e5. } 7... Nxe5 { The pawn is lost. } )
7... Nge7 { Heads for f5, again to put a third piece on d4. } 8. b4 { [%cal Gb4b5,Gc1b2] **Idea:** the pawn gains space,
takes c5 away from Black, and prepares b5 against the knight on c6, which would take an attacker off d4. }
8... Nf5 9. Bb2 Be7 10. Be2 O-O 11. O-O Bd7 { Both sides have developed; the pieces are in place for the standard
plans. } 12. Qd3 { The source gives the queen move without comment; it supports the Nbd2-b3 and b5 plan that
follows. }
( 12. g4 { A sharper try: the pawn attacks the knight on f5 directly. } 12... Nh4 13. Nbd2 Ng6 14. Nb3 a5 15. b5 Nf4
16. Bc1 Nxe2+ 17. Qxe2 Nb4 18. a4 Rfc8 19. Ba3 )
12... f6 13. Nbd2 fxe5 14. dxe5 Be8 15. Nb3 a5 16. b5 a4 { [%csl Rb3] The source stops here, with the knight on b3
attacked and the c6-knight about to lose its square. **Verdict:** a balanced middlegame. White has more space, but
...a4 gains time against the knight; the sources give no evaluation. } *

[Line "6.a3: 6...Bd7, 6...a5 and 6...f6"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.a3: the main line"]
[Summary "Black waits with ...Bd7, ...a5 or ...f6. Against ...Bd7 the pawn goes to b4; against ...a5 White can play the Milner-Barry set-up, because the a-pawn has left b5 undefended."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. a3 Bd7 { Delays the knight and keeps ...Qc7 available. }
7. b4 { [%cal Gb4c5] The point of a3: the pawn expands before Black has committed the knights. }
( 7. Bd3?! { Offering the Milner-Barry pawn with a3 and ...Bd7 already on the board is a different matter. The
Lichess study based on Pedersen's book has 7...cxd4 8.cxd4 Nxd4 9.Nxd4 Qxd4 10.Qe2 f6 11.Be3 Qxe5 and says that the
gambit failed. The real gambit lines are in the Milner-Barry file, with the bishop on d3 before a3. }
7... cxd4 8. cxd4 Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Qe2 f6 11. Be3 Qxe5 )
7... cxd4 8. cxd4 Rc8 { Black takes the open c-file at once. This is where Wikibooks stops; the pawn structure is
the one of the Lputian line, with Black's knight still on g8. The source gives no more moves, so this line is a
draft. } *

[Line "6.a3: 6...a5"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.a3: the main line"]
[Summary "6...a5 stops b4 for good but leaves b5 weak, and White switches to the Milner-Barry set-up."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. a3 a5 { [%csl Yb5] Black prevents b4 by force. The price: b5
can never be covered by a pawn again. } 7. Bd3 { Now the Milner-Barry gambit, with the extra moves a3 and ...a5
included (the study based on Pedersen's book). }
( 7. Be2 { The quiet continuation: Nc3-b5 uses the weak square. } 7... cxd4 8. cxd4 Nge7 9. Nc3 Nf5 10. Nb5 )
7... Bd7 8. O-O cxd4 9. cxd4 Nxd4 10. Nxd4 Qxd4 11. Nc3 Qxe5 12. Re1 Qb8 13. Nxd5 Bc6 14. Nb6 Nf6 15. Nxa8 Qxa8
{ White has won the exchange for a pawn and keeps the initiative; evaluation not given by the study. } *

[Line "6.a3 f6"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.a3: the main line"]
[Summary "Black strikes at e5 at once. White develops with Bd3 and meets ...c4 with Bc2."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. a3 f6 { [%cal Rf6e5] The break with ...f6, played before the
kingside is developed. Against 5...Bd7, Wikipedia says that 6.a3 f6 gives Black at least equality, because without the
queen on b6 the move a3 hits nothing. Here the queen is already committed. } 7. Bd3 { [%cal Gd3c2] The bishop develops. }
7... c4 8. Bc2 { **Idea:** the bishop keeps the b1-h7 diagonal, and a black knight cannot land on f5 without being
hit. The study based on Pedersen's book stops here, so this line is a draft. } *

[Line "Quiet alternative: 6.Be2 cxd4 7.cxd4 Nh6 8.Nc3 Nf5 9.Na4"]
[Section "5...Qb6: the quiet alternatives 6.Be2 and 6.Na3"]
[Summary "White simply develops and castles later. After the trade on d4 the knight jumps to a4 with tempo against the queen; this avoids the complications of 6.a3 c4."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. Be2 { [%cal Ge1g1,Gb1c3] The study with 395 likes calls this the
Paulsen Attack. White develops and waits for Black to take on d4; the knight then goes to c3-a4. It is the quiet
alternative to 6.a3 (Wikipedia, Marin chapters 6 and 7). }
( 6. Na3 { Marin's chapter 5 and Jobava's course. The knight defends d4 from c2; Marin's outline gives 6...cxd4 7.cxd4 Bd7
8.Nc2 Nb4 and an Efimenko-Fridman line, and the readable source stops at these moves. } 6... cxd4 7. cxd4 Bd7 8. Nc2 Nb4 )
6... cxd4 { Black takes. }
( 6... Nge7 { Levy's repertoire for Black (4231 likes) leaves the pawn alone and develops. } 7. dxc5 Qc7 { White takes while the
e7-knight blocks the f8-bishop, so the pawn cannot be recaptured at once. } )
( 6... Nh6 { Levy's other idea: the knight heads for f5. Wikipedia says White usually answers 7.Bxh6 or 7.b3 (preparing Bb2);
Levy gives 7.O-O. } 7. O-O
( 7. Bxh6?! { Gives up the bishop for a knight and helps Black open the g-file. } 7... gxh6 { Levy and Wikipedia (on the
similar 6.a3 line) call this good for Black: the g-file and the bishop White has given up. The sources disagree here. } )
7... cxd4 8. cxd4 Nf5 { The knight reaches f5 with a third attacker on d4. } )
7. cxd4 Nh6 { [%cal Gh6f5,Rf5d4] The usual idea: the knight goes to f5 to attack d4. On the chess.com forum ThrillerFan says
8.Bxh6 Qxb2 now troubles Black, Toldsted thinks Black can escape; the forum recommends 8.Nc3 Nf5 9.Na4 (played in
Nimzowitsch-Spielmann, 1920). }
( 7... Nge7 { Marin's version: the same knight route from e7. The forum warns that 8.O-O? allows ...Nf5, winning at least a
pawn. } 8. Nc3
( 8. Na3 { Marin's chapter 7: the knight goes to c2, where it protects d4. } 8... Nf5 9. Nc2 Bd7 10. O-O Rc8 )
8... Nf5 9. Na4 Qa5+ )
8. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3a4,Gc3b5] Develops with a threat: Na4 hits the queen. } 8... Nf5 9. Na4 { [%cal Ga4b6,Ga4c5] The queen has to leave
the b6-g1 diagonal. } 9... Qa5+ 10. Bd2 Bb4 11. Bc3 Bxc3+ 12. Nxc3 Qb6 13. Bb5 { [%cal Gb5c6] } 13... Bd7 14. Bxc6 Bxc6 15. Qd2 O-O
16. O-O { **Verdict:** the study with 395 likes plays the line without comment, and none of the readable
sources gives an evaluation, so treat this as a draft. } *
